# November 19, 2026: the Brookfields and Warren, Worcester County, Massachusetts (West Brookfield, Brookfield,
# North Brookfield, Warren). Region cmass drawn at random by tools/choose.py; the Atlas had nothing within 10 km of
# West Brookfield. Sources read in this session: Wikipedia articles through tools/wiki_raw.py; HMdb marker pages through
# tools/page.py; the West Brookfield Historical Commission's site (westbrookfield.org), The Trustees, the Merrick and
# Warren public libraries, the Salem Cross Inn, the American Heritage Foundation's North Brookfield Town House article and
# the Worcester Business Journal through tools/page.py.
# Pins: ArcGIS geocoder PointAddress results, HMdb marker positions and OpenStreetMap buildings, each looked at on the
# MassGIS 2023 leaf-off aerial with tools/pincheck.py.
from data_b import N
def W(t): return ['Wikipedia: '+t,'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/'+t.replace(' ','_')]
def L(a,b): return [a,b]
def HM(n,t): return ['HMdb: '+t,'https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=%d'%n]
BATCH={'key':'cmass_ikmd','label':'The Brookfields and Warren, Massachusetts','added':'2026-11-19 21:28'}
WBHC='West Brookfield Historical Commission'
PLACES=[
N('lucy-stone-home-site','Lucy Stone Home Site','West Brookfield','cmass','landmark','ruins',42.25645,-72.19296,'site','public','walk','','1818–1950',
  'Lucy Stone (1818-1893), speaker for women\'s rights and against slavery, was born in a farmhouse on the side of Coys Hill on August 13, 1818. She was the first Massachusetts woman to earn a college degree (1847), helped organize the first National Women\'s Rights Convention in 1850, and kept her own name when she married Henry Blackwell in 1855. The farmhouse burned in 1950. The site, 61 wooded acres, belongs to The Trustees as part of Rock House Reservation and was listed on the National Register in 2021.',
  look='The ruins of the burned farmhouse at the centre of the property, a granite marker stone and a large information sign put up by the Quaboag Historical Society. A Quaboag Historical Society marker stands where Coys Hill Road leaves Route 9. What is left of the foundations was not described in any source read.',
  accn='Owned by The Trustees (Rock House Reservation): daily, sunrise to sunset, free; dogs on leash; hunting allowed west of the Outer Loop only, not on Sundays (Trustees page, read November 2026). The Trustees say to follow the signs to the home site on Coy Hill Road, about a mile east of the Rock House main lot on Route 9 (12 cars); the town commission\'s page puts it 0.3 mile up Coy Hill Road from Route 9, on the right. Where to leave a car on Coy Hill Road itself was not checked. During Route 9 construction the Trustees ask visitors to use the Pierce Road lot.',
  links=[W('Lucy Stone Home Site'),L('The Trustees: Rock House Reservation','https://thetrustees.org/place/rock-house-reservation/'),L(WBHC+': Lucy Stone Homesite','https://westbrookfield.org/lucy-stone-homesite/'),HM(107668,'Birthplace of Lucy Stone')],
  th=[42.26806,-72.19853],thn='Rock House Reservation lot, Route 9 (about a mile from the home site)',brick='?'),
N('jedediah-foster-homesite','Jedediah Foster Homesite','West Brookfield','cmass','landmark','ruins',42.23264,-72.12853,'site','public','drive','','1735–1901',
  'Brigadier General Joseph Dwight built the house about 1735 and deeded it to his daughter Dorothy and her husband Jedediah Foster, married in 1749. Foster (1726-1779), a legislator from 1761, Revolutionary militia colonel and Superior Court justice, practised law in an office beside the house and sat on the committee that drafted the Massachusetts Constitution in 1779. The empty house and office burned in November 1901, and in 1902 the family gave the half-acre to the Quaboag Historical Society on condition that nothing be built there and it be kept open as a place of historic interest. National Register 2013.',
  look='Foundations of the house and the law office, two entry stones and part of a stone walkway, left much as they were after the 1901 fire; picnic tables, an information kiosk and a DAR marker ("Built 1735, Burned 1901") at the edge of the lot.',
  accn='Owned by the Quaboag Historical Society and kept open as a small park under the 1902 deed (town historical commission page, read November 2026). On Foster Hill Road at the top of the hill; parking not checked: look for room on the lawn edge or the road shoulder. The rest of Foster Hill is private property.',
  links=[W('Jedediah Foster Homesite'),L(WBHC+': Jedediah Foster Homesite','https://westbrookfield.org/jedediah-foster-homesite/'),HM(304199,'Judge Jebediah Foster')],brick='?'),
N('ayres-fortified-house-foster-hill','Quaboag Plantation sites, Foster Hill','West Brookfield','cmass','fort','marker',42.22925,-72.12350,'site','view','drive','forts','1665–1675',
  'Families from Ipswich settled Quaboag Plantation on Foster Hill in 1665; it was incorporated as the town of Brookfield in 1673. On August 2, 1675, in King Philip\'s War, the town was besieged: the inhabitants (ninety-nine people by the town commission\'s account) held out in John Ayres\'s fortified tavern while the houses and the new meeting house were burned, and they were relieved by troops from Marlborough three days later. The town was abandoned for decades. George Whitefield preached to a crowd of four or five hundred from a rock on the hill on October 16, 1740.',
  look='Stone markers along Foster Hill Road: "Here stood the fortified house besieged in King Philip\'s War, Aug. 2-4 1675" at a stone wall; "the first and second meeting houses" (the first burned August 4, 1675, the second dedicated 1717) about 300 feet away; the Millett home site, the first burial ground marker (the ground itself on a knoll 120 rods northeast), Whitefield Rock and the Foster homesite further west. The marked well and Indian Rock stand on private land and are seen from the wall.',
  accn='The commission says the Foster Hill area is private property; respect "No Trespassing" signs. The markers are read from Foster Hill Road (the fortified-house stone is just west of George Allen Road, near 80 Foster Hill Road, by HMdb). No parking area named.',
  links=[L(WBHC+': Foster Hill','https://westbrookfield.org/foster-hill/'),HM(311571,'The Fortified House'),HM(304101,'The First and Second Meeting Houses'),HM(304114,'First Burial Ground'),L(WBHC+': Whitefield Rock','https://westbrookfield.org/whitefield-rock/')],brick='no'),
N('fort-gilbert-west-brookfield','Fort Gilbert site','West Brookfield','cmass','fort','marker',42.23838,-72.13578,'area','public','drive','forts','1688–1710s',
  'After reports of killings at Northfield in 1688, Major Pynchon sent men to fortify the second settlement of Brookfield, and the carpenter Thomas Gilbert built the stockade that became known as Gilbert\'s Fort. It was repaired in 1702 and was still serviceable in the French and Indian wars; Sabbath services seem to have been held there until 1713. A 1930 Tercentenary marker stands near the site.',
  look='Nothing of the fort: a 1930 Massachusetts Bay Colony Tercentenary marker, restored in 2019. The commission says the fort stood on the old centre schoolhouse lot at North Main and Maple Streets, and the present elementary school stands on land that was inside the stockade.',
  accn='Roadside marker on a public street (HMdb position); the school grounds are the town\'s, so look from the street on school days.',
  links=[L(WBHC+': Fort Gilbert','https://westbrookfield.org/fort-gilbert/'),HM(48782,'Fort Gilbert')],brick='no'),
N('old-indian-cemetery-west-brookfield','Old Indian Cemetery','West Brookfield','cmass','burial','standing',42.23697,-72.14572,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1710–1849',
  'West Brookfield\'s old burying ground, in use from 1710 to 1849 and listed on the National Register in 2006. It holds 16 French and Indian War soldiers, 11 Revolutionary War soldiers, the six "Haymakers" killed in an attack in 1710, Judge Jedediah Foster with his wife and daughter, and Diederick Leertouwer.',
  look='A rectangle walled in stone on all sides with one walk-in entrance on Cottage Street; level grass under scattered large pines, no shrubs, and a monument to the Haymakers. The commission notes conservation work under way on the stones.',
  accn='Old burying ground at 50 Cottage Street, entered on foot through its one opening on the street (town historical commission page, read November 2026). Hours not posted on any page read; visit in daylight.',
  links=[W('Old Indian Cemetery'),L(WBHC+': Old Indian Cemetery','https://westbrookfield.org/old-indian-cemetary/')],brick='?'),
N('salem-cross-inn','Salem Cross Inn (White Homestead)','West Brookfield','cmass','hotel','standing',42.24414,-72.17319,'exact','open','drive','hotels','c. 1707, 1740',
  'Sergeant John White, a grandson of the Mayflower-born Peregrine White, was granted this land in 1707 and built a one-storey house; he was killed in an attack in 1710. His sons John and Cornelius built the present hip-roofed Georgian house and barn about 1740. It is now a restaurant and tavern on a 600-acre farm, listed on the National Register in 1975 and 1978.',
  look='A two-storey clapboard Georgian house with a large central chimney, its ells running back to the barn. Inside, original floors and woodwork and much of the original plaster survive (Register summary). The c. 1707 first house, its chimney gone, was made a carriage shed and moved behind the main house about 1960. In winter the inn serves "Fireplace Feasts" cooked on its open hearth.',
  accn='A working restaurant at 260 West Main Street (Route 9); the dining rooms are seen by eating there. Hours are from the inn\'s own page (read November 2026), which asks for reservations by phone; its winter hearth dinners are ticketed.',
  links=[W('Salem Cross Inn'),L('Salem Cross Inn','https://www.salemcrossinn.com/'),L(WBHC+': Salem Cross Inn','https://westbrookfield.org/salem-cross-inn/')],
  hours='Thursday to Saturday 4 to 8 p.m.; Sunday noon to 5 p.m.; closed Monday to Wednesday',brick='some'),
N('hitchcock-tavern-west-brookfield','Hitchcock Tavern (Ye Olde Tavern)','West Brookfield','cmass','hotel','standing',42.23474,-72.14157,'exact','view','drive','hotels','1760',
  'Captain David Hitchcock built the tavern in 1760 on the Bay Path, the stage road between Boston and Albany. George Washington stopped here in 1789, and its owners count John Adams, Daniel Shays and Lafayette among its guests. Long run as Ye Olde Tavern, it reopened under its original name, Hitchcock Tavern, after that business closed (Worcester Business Journal article, undated in the copy read; the tavern was then 263 years old).',
  look='The 1760 tavern building at 7 East Main Street, facing the West Brookfield common. Interior not checked.',
  accn='Privately owned restaurant building. Whether a restaurant is serving there today was not checked: no page of its own was found, so treat it as a building seen from the street and the common.',
  links=[L('Worcester Business Journal: tavern reopens','https://www.wbjournal.com/article/historic-tavern-which-served-founding-fathers-reopens-in-west-brookfield'),L(WBHC+': Ye Olde Tavern','https://westbrookfield.org/ye-olde-tavern/')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('west-brookfield-railroad-depots','West Brookfield railroad depots','West Brookfield','cmass','rail','standing',42.22975,-72.14507,'site','open','walk','stations','1847, 1884',
  'The Western Railroad reached West Brookfield in 1839. A larger station replaced the first one in 1847, and a Greek Revival freight house was built the same year. In 1884 the Boston & Albany built a Richardsonian Romanesque depot on Front Street, designed by Eugene C. Gardner, and moved the 1847 station to Ware Street. The town commission\'s consultant counts the two 1847 buildings among the oldest railroad buildings in the country; Wikipedia dates the older depot to between 1839 and 1847.',
  look='The 1884 stone-and-shingle style depot, once the senior centre and now the museum of the Quaboag Historical Society; the 1847 Gothic Revival station on Ware Street; and the 1847 freight house, intact. All three are in the 2006 enlargement of the centre historic district. Interior of the museum not checked.',
  accn='The 1884 depot is the Quaboag Historical Society\'s museum on Front Street; no page read gave its opening hours. The 1847 station and freight house are seen from the street. The line is the active CSX main line: stay off the tracks.',
  links=[W('West Brookfield Center Historic District'),L(WBHC+': West Brookfield Rail Road Station','https://westbrookfield.org/biographies/railroad-3/')],
  conf='mixed',hours='?',brick='?'),
N('brookfield-cemetery','Brookfield Cemetery','Brookfield','cmass','burial','standing',42.21722,-72.11361,'site','public','drive','cemeteries','1714',
  'Church members set aside the first burial plot here in 1714; the town took over the oldest five acres in 1760. It is the town\'s only cemetery, 43 acres with about 10,000 marked graves, including founders of the towns once part of Brookfield. National Register 2003.',
  look='Three phases side by side: the 18th-century west end, a Victorian rural-cemetery middle section with a stone wall of the 1850s, an 1861 receiving tomb, a large granite entrance gate of 1873 given by William and Otis Hayden Banister, and a Civil War memorial dedicated in 1890; and the 20th-century east end.',
  accn='Town cemetery on Main Street (Route 9) at the west side of Brookfield. The town\'s cemetery page answered 403, so hours and rules were not checked; visit in daylight.',
  links=[W('Brookfield Cemetery'),HM(106166,'Civil War Memorial')],brick='?'),
N('brookfield-common','Brookfield Common','Brookfield','cmass','town','standing',42.21531,-72.10238,'site','public','walk','','1735',
  'Brookfield\'s centre grew round a common laid out in 1735 as a militia training ground, where the Third Parish church was set up. The Brookfield Common Historic District (National Register 1990) holds more than 100 properties: Federal houses of the 1790s, the oldest in the village; the Romanesque Revival Congregational church of 1857 by Boyden & Ball; Banister Memorial Hall (1883-84, the library); and the Colonial Revival town hall of 1904 by George H. Clemence.',
  look='The common with its war memorials (HMdb), the 1857 church at its north end, the library at the south corner, the town hall, and rows of early houses on the roads east of the common. Interior of the church not checked.',
  accn='Town common, open ground. Street parking on Lincoln and Common Streets (library\'s page asks drivers to park on the common side only).',
  links=[W('Brookfield Common Historic District'),HM(198791,'Brookfield Civil War Memorial')],brick='?'),
N('merrick-public-library-brookfield','Merrick Public Library (Banister Memorial Hall)','Brookfield','cmass','civic','standing',42.21369,-72.10252,'exact','open','walk','','1883–1884',
  'Justice Pliny Merrick founded the library in 1867 with $10,000 and his own books. Nancy Felton gave the corner lot for $1 in 1883, and William A. Banister paid for the building, designed by Wait & Cutter of Boston and dedicated on January 31, 1884 as Banister Memorial Hall.',
  look='Romanesque and Queen Anne masonry of bricks made in Brookfield, on a faced granite base, trimmed with Longmeadow brownstone and terra cotta; inside, shelves, cases and desks finished in birch and an 1898 gallery over the main book room. The library shows a French writing desk said to come from Versailles, given to the town in 1867 (library history page).',
  accn='Town library at 2 Lincoln Street, open to all; closed on holidays and on a Saturday before a Monday holiday. Street parking on Lincoln Street and on the common side of Common Street; step-free entrance on Lincoln Street (library\'s page, read November 2026).',
  links=[L('Merrick Public Library: History','https://merrickpubliclibrary.org/history'),L('Merrick Public Library: Hours','https://www.merrickpubliclibrary.org/hours-contact'),HM(198788,'Banister Memorial Hall')],
  hours='Tuesday and Thursday 1 to 8 p.m.; Wednesday and Friday 11 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Saturday 10 a.m. to 1 p.m.; closed Sunday and Monday',brick='yes'),
N('elm-hill-farm-brookfield','Elm Hill Farm','Brookfield','cmass','landmark','standing',42.22486,-72.08780,'area','view','drive','','Federal period on',
  'For over a century one of the largest and most important farms in Brookfield. The historic district (National Register 1991) takes in two Federal-period houses, two with Queen Anne styling and four Italianate outbuildings. Elsie the Cow was first kept here.',
  look='The farm complex on East Main Street east of Brookfield Road: the houses and Italianate barns and outbuildings, seen from the road.',
  accn='Private farm: view from East Main Street (Route 9) only.',
  links=[W('Elm Hill Farm Historic District')],brick='?'),
N('camp-atwater','Camp Atwater','North Brookfield','cmass','inst','standing',42.23944,-72.05,'site','view','drive','','1921 (White Cabin c. 1760)',
  'Dr. William N. DeBerry founded St. John\'s Camp on the north shore of Lake Lashaway in 1921 to give African-American boys a summer camp when camps were generally segregated; it took the Atwater name after Mary Atwater\'s gift in 1926. Its campers came from up and down the Atlantic coast and include Coleman Young, Clifford Alexander Jr., Clifton Wharton Jr. and Hazel O\'Leary. National Register 1982; now owned and run by the Urban League of Springfield.',
  look='Main hall, recreation and dining halls and fourteen cabins round a U-shaped drive between Shore Road and the lake. The oldest building is the White Cabin of about 1760, on the north side of Shore Road, where the first season was held.',
  accn='A working private camp: look from Shore Road only.',
  links=[W('Camp Atwater')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('north-brookfield-town-house','North Brookfield Town House','North Brookfield','cmass','civic','standing',42.26876,-72.0854,'exact','view','walk','','1864',
  'The town\'s third town hall, built in 1864 to designs by E. Boyden & Son, with Italianate and Second Empire decoration and a 60-foot corner tower. Over the years it was town hall, theatre, shops and jail. It has stood empty since 2002; the Friends of the North Brookfield Town House, founded in 2006, stabilised the roof and bell tower and repainted the front, and plan a Creative Life Center for the arts there (American Heritage Foundation article, undated). National Register 2001.',
  look='The wooden building with tall arched windows and its bell tower at North Main and Summer Streets, the centre of the business district. Interior not checked; whether any part is open now was not checked.',
  accn='Town-owned building on the main street; seen from the sidewalk.',
  links=[W('North Brookfield Town House'),L('American Heritage Foundation: North Brookfield Town House','https://ahfboston.com/?p=627')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('crossman-bridge-warren','Crossman Bridge','Warren','cmass','landmark','standing',42.21028,-72.24556,'site','public','drive','','1888',
  'Built in 1888 by the Berlin Iron Bridge Co. for $2,175, the first metal bridge at a crossing used since 1795. It is the only known lenticular pony truss bridge (its curved trusses are not joined over the roadway) and one of three lenticular bridges in Massachusetts. It was taken down, repaired and put back by U.S. Bridge of Cambridge, Ohio, between 2004 and 2008. Named for the A. W. Crossman factory that stood south of the river. National Register 2010.',
  look='A 73-foot iron span with lens-shaped trusses about 8 feet deep, on concrete abutments capped with granite, and its old decorative railing behind a modern guard rail.',
  accn='Carries Gilbert Road over the Quaboag River just south of Route 67, west of West Warren; a public road. No parking area mapped: pull off only where the shoulder allows.',
  links=[W('Crossman Bridge')],brick='no'),
N('warren-public-library','Warren Public Library','Warren','cmass','civic','standing',42.21355,-72.19304,'exact','open','walk','','1889',
  'The library was chartered in 1876 and opened in rooms of the town hall in 1879. Wilson H. Fairbank gave the land for this building, designed by Amos P. Cutting in the Richardsonian Romanesque style and built in 1889. The upstairs, meant for a lecture hall, became a museum and later stacks; it was shut in the Second World War and reopened in 2001. National Register 2000.',
  look='Granite banded with brownstone under a slate roof, with a three-storey pyramid-roofed tower, a bay window, round-arched openings and a small arcade below the eaves.',
  accn='Town library at 934 Main Street, open to all (hours from the library\'s own page, read November 2026).',
  links=[W('Warren Public Library (Warren, Massachusetts)'),L('Warren Public Library: Hours','https://warrenpubliclibrary.org/hours/')],
  hours='Tuesday and Thursday 11 a.m. to 7 p.m.; Wednesday and Saturday 11 a.m. to 3 p.m.; closed Sunday, Monday and Friday',brick='no'),
N('warren-first-congregational-church','Warren First Congregational Church (Emmanuel Orthodox Church)','Warren','cmass','worship','standing',42.21368,-72.19185,'exact','view','walk','','1875',
  'Built in 1875 to Amos P. Cutting\'s design for Warren\'s first congregation, on the site to which its 1797 meeting house had been moved in the 1830s; that building burned in 1874. Since 2008 it has been Emmanuel Orthodox Church, a parish of the Antiochian Orthodox archdiocese. National Register 2004.',
  look='A Victorian Gothic wooden church with stained glass by Samuel West; the congregation keeps the building and its 1875 Steer & Turner pipe organ (Wikipedia). Interior and service times not checked.',
  accn='Active church at 25 Winthrop Terrace; no page of its own was found with service times, so see it from the street.',
  links=[W('Warren First Congregational Church-Federated Church')],brick='no'),
]
